Give java.lang.Enum its no-argument constructor whichever root loads it

An enum may write its parent as `java.lang.Enum[X]`, without the name and
ordinal that `CompleteJavaEnums` adds later. `Definitions` makes that typecheck
by replacing the loader of `java.lang.Enum` with one that, after reading the
class file, strips the parameters from its primary constructor and enters a
copy with both. But the class file is read for the class and its companion
together, by whichever is completed first, and only the class got the new
loader. A reference to a static member, such as `java.lang.Enum.valueOf`,
completes the companion first, the class is left with only its two-parameter
constructor, and an enum typed after it fails with "not enough arguments" from
source, or "wrong number of arguments" when it is read from TASTy after a unit
which uses the static member.

The module class now gets the new loader too, as a proxy, and the constructors
are adjusted in `doComplete`, which runs once for both roots.

